sections.body:
A **functional interface** declares exactly one **abstract** method. It may also have any number of `default` and `static` methods, since they already have bodies, but only one method may be left for someone else to implement.

```java
@FunctionalInterface
interface Greeter {
    String greet(String name);   // the ONE abstract method

    default Greeter shouting() {
        return name -> greet(name).toUpperCase();   // default method, doesn't count against the limit
    }
}
```

`@FunctionalInterface` is optional. It doesn't change how the interface behaves, but it makes the compiler verify the one-abstract-method shape and fail if someone later adds a second one, a cheap safeguard on any interface meant to be implemented with a lambda. `Runnable` (one method, `run()`) and `Comparator<T>` (one abstract method, `compare(T, T)`, plus default methods like `reversed()` and `thenComparing()`) are functional interfaces from the standard library, and both predate lambdas: lambdas needed no new interfaces, only a shorter way to implement the existing single-method ones. The one-abstract-method rule follows from how lambdas work. A lambda body has no method name attached, so the compiler needs exactly one method signature to bind it to. With two abstract methods there would be no answer to 'which one does this body implement?'

When a lambda's body only calls one existing method, a **method reference** (`::`) says the same thing without a parameter name that exists just to be forwarded. There are four forms:

```java
// 1. Static method reference
Function<String, Integer> parse = Integer::parseInt;          // same as: s -> Integer.parseInt(s)

// 2. Instance method reference on a PARTICULAR, already-existing object
List<String> names = new ArrayList<>();
Consumer<String> adder = names::add;                          // same as: s -> names.add(s)

// 3. Instance method reference on an ARBITRARY object of a given type
Function<String, Integer> len = String::length;                // same as: s -> s.length()
// here, the lambda's own parameter becomes the object the method is called ON

// 4. Constructor reference
Supplier<List<String>> factory = ArrayList::new;               // same as: () -> new ArrayList<>()
```

The third form looks confusing at first because `String::length` shows no object to call the method on. When it is used as a `Function<String, Integer>`, the single incoming parameter becomes the receiver that `.length()` is called on. All four forms are shorthand: anywhere you can write a method reference you could write the equivalent lambda by hand. They just remove ceremony when a lambda would do nothing beyond forwarding to one method.

A lambda body can *read* local variables and parameters from its enclosing scope, with one firm restriction: a captured local variable must be **effectively final**, meaning it is never reassigned after its initial assignment, even if it isn't declared `final`.

```java
int total = 0;
Runnable r = () -> {
    total++;                      // does NOT compile: total is reassigned, so it's not effectively final
};
```

The restriction exists because a lambda can outlive the method call that created it (stored in a field, passed to another thread, invoked much later), and Java avoids the 'which value did it capture, and when' ambiguity by disallowing capture of anything that changes. When a lambda needs to accumulate state, capture a reference to a mutable *container* instead: a one-element array or, more idiomatically (and safely across threads), an `AtomicInteger` or `AtomicReference`. The *reference* stays effectively final while the container's contents change.

`this` also differs between a lambda and an old-style anonymous inner class. A lambda introduces no instance of its own, so `this` in a lambda body means what it means at that point in the enclosing code, as if the body were written inline. An anonymous inner class creates its own object with its own `this`, so reaching the enclosing instance from inside it takes the more verbose `EnclosingClass.this.method()`. That is a practical advantage of lambdas whenever a callback needs to call back into the surrounding object.

