sections.body:
Calling an intermediate operation processes no elements; it just records a step in the pipeline. Nothing happens until a terminal operation is invoked, and then elements are pulled through the whole chain, generally one at a time, from source to terminal.

```java
List<String> names = List.of("amy", "ben", "cara");

names.stream()
    .map(n -> {
        System.out.println("mapping " + n);
        return n.toUpperCase();
    });
// prints nothing: there is no terminal operation, so the pipeline never runs

List<String> upper = names.stream()
    .map(n -> {
        System.out.println("mapping " + n);
        return n.toUpperCase();
    })
    .collect(java.util.stream.Collectors.toList());
// now it prints "mapping amy", "mapping ben", "mapping cara": collect() is a terminal operation
```

Laziness is what lets Java stop early for `findFirst()` or `anyMatch()` without processing the rest of a large or even infinite source: the pipeline pulls only as many elements as the terminal operation needs. The flip side is that a chain of intermediate operations with no terminal operation compiles fine and does nothing at run time, an easy no-op to leave behind if you forget the final `.collect(...)` or `.forEach(...)`. One more caution: a terminal operation is also free to skip work it can answer without running the pipeline. Since Java 9, `count()` on a stream whose size is known from the source (a `List`, say) and not changed by the operations (`map` keeps the size) just returns the size, so a `peek` or side-effecting `map` before it never runs. Put side effects in `forEach`, not in `map`.

`collect(Collector)` is the general-purpose terminal operation for gathering a stream's elements into a structure. The `Collectors` utility class supplies ready-made collectors for most cases: `Collectors.toList()` / `toSet()` for the obvious cases, `Collectors.toMap(keyFn, valueFn)` for building a `Map`, `Collectors.joining(delimiter)` for concatenating a stream of `String`s, and `Collectors.groupingBy(classifierFn)` for partitioning elements into a `Map<K, List<T>>` keyed by whatever the classifier function computes.

```java
List<String> words = List.of("pear", "fig", "kiwi", "plum", "date");

Map<Integer, List<String>> byLength = words.stream()
    .collect(java.util.stream.Collectors.groupingBy(String::length));
// {3=[fig], 4=[pear, kiwi, plum, date]}

String joined = words.stream()
    .collect(java.util.stream.Collectors.joining(", "));
System.out.println(joined);   // pear, fig, kiwi, plum, date
```

One last rule: a `Stream` can be traversed only **once**. A terminal operation consumes the stream, and any further operation on that reference (another `collect`, another `forEach`, anything) throws `IllegalStateException: stream has already been operated upon or closed`. To run a pipeline again, call `.stream()` again on the original source to get a new stream.

examples.code:
import java.util.List;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class FullPipeline {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> words = List.of("pear", "fig", "kiwi", "plum", "date", "apple");

        List<String> result = words.stream()
            .filter(w -> w.length() > 3)
            .map(String::toUpperCase)
            .sorted()
            .collect(Collectors.toList());

        System.out.println(result);   // [APPLE, DATE, KIWI, PEAR, PLUM]
    }
}

import java.util.List;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class LazinessDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> names = List.of("amy", "ben", "cara");

        System.out.println("building pipeline with no terminal operation...");
        names.stream()
            .map(n -> {
                System.out.println("mapping " + n);
                return n.toUpperCase();
            });
        System.out.println("...nothing printed above this line from map()");

        System.out.println("now adding a terminal operation:");
        List<String> upper = names.stream()
            .map(n -> {
                System.out.println("mapping " + n);
                return n.toUpperCase();
            })
            .collect(Collectors.toList());
        System.out.println("result = " + upper);
    }
}

import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class ReduceVsCollect {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> words = List.of("pear", "fig", "kiwi", "plum", "date");

        int totalLength = words.stream()
            .map(String::length)
            .reduce(0, Integer::sum);
        System.out.println("total length: " + totalLength);   // 4+3+4+4+4 = 19

        Map<Integer, List<String>> byLength = words.stream()
            .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(String::length));
        System.out.println(byLength);   // {3=[fig], 4=[pear, kiwi, plum, date]}
    }
}

