sections.body:
`Optional.of(value)` wraps a value that must not be `null` (throwing `NullPointerException` immediately if it is); `Optional.ofNullable(value)` is the safer choice when the value might be `null`, producing an empty `Optional` in that case instead of throwing; `Optional.empty()` represents 'nothing here.'

`get()` throws `NoSuchElementException` if the `Optional` is empty, so calling it without checking presence reproduces the bug `Optional` exists to prevent under a different exception name. The idiomatic alternatives read better and are safer:

```java
Optional<User> maybeUser = findUserById(42);

String name = maybeUser.map(User::getName).orElse("Unknown");

User user = maybeUser.orElseThrow(() -> new NoSuchElementException("No user with id 42"));

maybeUser.ifPresent(u -> System.out.println("Found: " + u.getName()));
```

`orElse(defaultValue)` and `orElseGet(supplier)` look alike but differ in one important way. `orElse`'s argument is evaluated *eagerly*, every time, even when the `Optional` has a value and the default is discarded; `orElseGet`'s supplier is invoked *only if the Optional is empty*. If the default is cheap (a literal, an existing object) you won't see a difference. If it is expensive (a database query, a fresh allocation), `orElse` does that work on every call, present or not, so use `orElseGet` whenever the fallback costs anything to produce.

A **compact constructor** lets you add validation or normalization logic without repeating the full parameter list or the field assignments, which happen implicitly right after the compact constructor's own body finishes:

```java
record Range(int start, int end) {
    public Range {                       // no parameter list repeated -- it's implicit
        if (start > end) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("start must not exceed end");
        }
    }                                     // this.start = start; this.end = end; happens automatically after this
}
```

Records can also declare additional constructors (which must ultimately delegate to the canonical one) and additional instance or static methods beyond the generated accessors, like a regular class.

One sharp edge to know before it surprises you: if a record component is an **array**, the generated `equals()` and `hashCode()` compare it by **reference identity**, not content. They do not use `Arrays.equals`; they effectively delegate to the component's own `equals` and `hashCode`, and arrays never override those from `Object`. So `record Buffer(byte[] data) { }` does *not* get value equality for `data`: two `Buffer`s wrapping arrays with identical bytes are not `.equals()`, the same pitfall plain arrays have outside records.

